#
# init.xrom.sentinel.rc — installed to /system_ext/etc/init/init.xrom.sentinel.rc
#

on post-fs-data
    # Sentinel state: incident records, the staged evidence copy, the lockdown marker
    # and the quarantine notice.
    #
    # 0700 system:system, same reasoning as /data/misc/xrom/avf. The incident log is
    # the audit trail for a security response and the staged evidence is the thing the
    # response exists to preserve; a directory either of them can be read from or
    # written to by anything else makes both worthless. sepolicy enforces the same
    # boundary by label, and both halves are needed: the mode bits survive a policy
    # mistake and the label survives a chmod.
    mkdir /data/misc/xrom/sentinel 0700 system system
    mkdir /data/misc/xrom/sentinel/incidents 0700 system system
    restorecon_recursive /data/misc/xrom/sentinel

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# xrom_sentineld — self-healing recovery daemon
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# class late_start rather than core: it needs /data mounted and it performs boot-time
# integrity work, neither of which belongs in the pre-data boot phase. It still runs
# early enough that a device which should not have come up at all does not spend its
# first seconds serving binder calls.
#
# CAP_NET_ADMIN is granted for exactly one reason: the SIOCSIFFLAGS layer of the
# network cut. It is the layer that works when netd does not, which on a kernel built
# with CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL off is an open question rather than an assumption. No other
# capability is granted and sepolicy has a neverallow for the rest.
#
# No `seclabel`: the domain transition is derived from the binary's label in
# file_contexts by init_daemon_domain(xrom_sentineld), which keeps working when the
# binary moves. A hard-coded seclabel would not.
#
# No `critical`: a crash loop here must not reboot the phone during bring-up. The
# responses are fail-closed at the daemon level instead — an unauthorised reporter is
# refused, an unreadable vault is inconclusive rather than a mismatch, and a network
# cut that no layer confirms is reported as not cut.
#
# `shutdown critical` IS set, and it is not a contradiction with the above. Rebooting
# is not an exceptional condition for this daemon; it is the last step of a plan. Being
# killed halfway through arming a BCB is how a device ends up with a partially written
# misc, which is worse than not rebooting at all. main() also ignores SIGTERM for the
# same reason, and both halves are needed: this entry keeps init from killing it early,
# the handler covers anything else that signals it.
service xrom_sentineld /system_ext/bin/xrom_sentineld
    class late_start
    user system
    group system
    capabilities NET_ADMIN
    task_profiles ServiceCapacityLow
    shutdown critical
